Here is the provided data.

<fuzz-target>
#include <fuzzer/FuzzedDataProvider.h>
#include <cstddef>
#include <cstdint>
#include <vector>

#include "strtod.h"
#include "utils.h"

extern "C" int LLVMFuzzerTestOneInput(const uint8_t* data, size_t size) {
FuzzedDataProvider provider(data, size);

// Constrain the exponent to the valid range [-1074, 971] as per the assertion.
const int exponent = provider.ConsumeIntegralInRange<int>(-1074, 971);
std::vector<char> buffer_vec = provider.ConsumeRemainingBytes<char>();

double_conversion::Vector<const char> buffer(
buffer_vec.data(), static_cast<int>(buffer_vec.size()));

double_conversion::Strtod(buffer, exponent);

return 0;
}
</fuzz-target>

<function-requirements>

<input>
project name: double-conversion
function signature: double double_conversion::Strtod(Vector<const char>, int)
</input>

<description>
The `Strtod` function is part of the `double-conversion` library and is responsible for converting a string representation of a floating-point number into a `double` type. It takes a `Vector<const char>` which encapsulates the input string and its length, and an integer `exponent`. The function first preprocesses the input string by calling the internal `TrimAndCut` function. This helper function trims leading and trailing whitespace and truncates the string to a maximum of `kMaxSignificantDecimalDigits` (780) significant digits. The trimmed and potentially truncated string, along with an updated exponent, is then passed to the `StrtodTrimmed` function, which performs the core conversion logic. The analysis of its usage within the project's test suite reveals that it is intended to be called with various numeric strings and a wide range of exponents.
</description>

<requirements>
<requirement>
The `buffer` argument, a `Vector<const char>`, should be initialized from fuzzer-generated data. This is necessary because the function is designed to parse potentially user-controlled string inputs.
</requirement>
<requirement>
If the `buffer.length_` is not zero, the `buffer.start_` pointer must be a valid pointer to a character array. A null pointer with a non-zero length will trigger an assertion failure in the `Vector` class constructor.
</requirement>
<requirement>
The `exponent` argument should be fuzzed with a wide range of integer values. The function's test cases show that it is expected to handle both large positive and negative exponents.
</requirement>
<requirement>
The length of the character array backing the `buffer` can be of any size. The `TrimAndCut` function internally handles strings longer than `kMaxSignificantDecimalDigits` (780) by truncating them.
</requirement>
</requirements>

</function-requirements>

